  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist in Kibana. The rule is enabled and its first run succeeded.\n\n**Data exploration (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, about 14,000 docs)**\n- Key fields: `timestamp`, `response` (string, aggregate via `response.keyword`), `bytes`, `clientip`, `url`/`request`, `geo.dest`, `agent`, and `host`.\n- Status codes: 200 (12,832), 404 (801), 503 (441). The only 5xx code in the data is 503.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-668f82d7 Web logs overview`** (id `308cc194-c0d5-43b6-9388-a57c0bd41f2b`)\n- Markdown header.\n- Metric: Total requests (count).\n- Metric: Total bytes transferred (sum of `bytes`).\n- Metric: Unique visitors (unique count of `clientip`).\n- Line chart: Requests over time (count, date histogram on `timestamp`).\n\nI read the title back from the API and it has all five panels.\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-668f82d7 5xx spike`** (id `26d1badd-c71b-4a72-8161-cd0b0c61f7f1`)\n- Type: Elasticsearch query rule, using a KQL search on the data view.\n- Query: `response.keyword >= \"500\" and response.keyword <= \"599\"`.\n- Schedule: every 1 minute. Window: 5 minutes. Count of all matches.\n- Condition: fires when count > 10. Enabled: yes.\n- The rule's last run succeeded with no alerts. The 5-minute window currently holds no 5xx matches, so it hasn't fired.\n- I checked the query's logic with an equivalent range filter on the same index, which returns 441 hits, matching the 503 count.\n\n**Choices I made**\n- The dashboard uses only the panel types and operations the spec validates. Average bytes isn't a valid metric operation for this panel type, so I used the sum instead. The ES|QL line chart from the spec example was rejected, so the line chart uses the data-view form.\n- The rule has no actions attached, so it will not notify anyone. It only evaluates and records alerts. Tell me if you want an email, Slack, or server-log connector added.",
